 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Moving Objects:** Outliers from moving objects (e.g., other vehicles, pedestrians) are handled by the confidence-guided learning and instance segmentation/optical flow removal [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Hardware Scaling:** Training time varies significantly by component; vehicles are faster (~2 hours/vehicle) than background scenes (~17 hours/scene) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Moving Objects:** Moving objects (e.g., other vehicles, pedestrians) must be removed via instance segmentation and optical flow before training the background model to prevent ghosting [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Pose Outliers:** Without confidence updating, shape-radiance ambiguity can cause outlier poses to exhibit higher rendering quality (PSNR) than inlier poses, leading to incorrect ray sampling [S1, unofficial]. The confidence map computation mitigates this.
+*   **Hardware Scaling:** Training time scales with scene complexity. Background scenes with ~250 images (1280×1920) require two RTX 3090 GPUs and ~17 hours, while foreground vehicles require one RTX 3090 and ~2 hours [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Confidence Map Computation
-Computes geometry and projection confidence maps to reduce the influence of depth outliers during training [S1, unofficial]. It projects 3D LiDAR points from time $t-1$ and $t+1$ to time $t$ using displacement $\Delta P$ between frames [S1, unofficial]. Consistency checks using optical flow and depth projection remove moving object outliers [S1, unofficial]. RGB, SSIM, and VGG confidence maps measure photometric and feature consistency [S1, unofficial]. These components are combined via learnable aggregation to produce final confidence maps [S1, unofficial]. The confidence threshold $\tau$ is set to 20% for background scenes [S1, unofficial].
+### [Confidence Updating with Detached Color Network](16065-confidence-updating-with-detached-color-network.md)
+This method addresses pose outlier contamination in neural surface reconstruction. It distinguishes itself by employing a detached color network ($C_n$) that excludes viewing direction inputs to mitigate shape-radiance ambiguity. Its output is used to dynamically update confidence scores for pose weighting during training.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training takes approximately 2 hours per vehicle on a single RTX 3090 GPU and ~17 hours per background scene (250 images, 1280×1920) on two RTX 3090 GPUs [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Total learnable parameters are 8.76M, similar to other SOTA methods (8.7–9.9M) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** The method achieves a PSNR of 23.60 and SSIM of 0.743 on the Waymo dataset, outperforming Mip-NeRF 360 (PSNR 22.10, SSIM 0.724) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Effective handling of dynamic street scenes using LiDAR guidance; robust against depth outliers via confidence maps.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires LiDAR data; training background scenes is computationally expensive compared to methods without coarse rendering supervision (e.g., Mip-NeRF 360 is faster as it skips coarse rendering) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training takes approximately 2 hours per foreground vehicle on a single RTX 3090 GPU and ~17 hours per background scene (250 images, 1280×1920) on two RTX 3090 GPUs [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** The total number of learnable parameters is 8.76M, which is comparable to other state-of-the-art methods (8.7–9.9M) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** On the Waymo dataset, S-NeRF achieves a PSNR of 23.60 and SSIM of 0.743, outperforming Mip-NeRF 360 (PSNR 22.10, SSIM 0.724) and Urban-NeRF (PSNR 17.80, SSIM 0.494) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Robust to LiDAR outliers via confidence maps; handles dynamic scenes by separating foreground and background.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires LiDAR data for confidence computation; training time is longer than methods that skip coarse rendering supervision (e.g., Mip-NeRF 360 is faster as it does not require coarse rendering for supervision) [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-S-NeRF was introduced in the paper "S-NeRF: Neural Radiance Fields for Street Views" published at ICLR 2023 [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the challenge of reconstructing large-scale dynamic street scenes by combining LiDAR depth information with RGB imagery, leveraging confidence maps to handle moving objects and depth outliers [S1, unofficial].
+S-NeRF was introduced in the paper "S-NeRF: Neural Radiance Fields for Street Views" published at ICLR 2023 [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to address the challenges of reconstructing large-scale dynamic street scenes using sparse LiDAR and RGB data, building upon the mip-NeRF optimization framework [S1, unofficial].
 
